    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> grew out of the Carolingian reorganisation of the south-west after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>’s campaigns in Aquitaine and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. In 778 Charlemagne appointed a Frankish noble, Chorson, as count of Toulouse; after Chorson was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> and deposed in 790, the county passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-gellone">William of Gellone</a>, a kinsman of the king, who defended the Spanish March against Umayyad raids and took part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801. William later retired to the monastery he had founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gellone-abbey">Gellone</a>, now Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert, and was venerated as a saint. Through the ninth century the county was contested among Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, but from the 840s a family descended from Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredelo">Fredelo</a> and his brother Raymond I established a hereditary hold over Toulouse and Rouergue. Historians call this line the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymondines">Raymondines</a>, after the name its counts used for generations. As royal authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> weakened, the counts gathered rights over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quercy">Quercy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, and in the tenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-pons">Raymond III Pons</a> appears in some documents with the title of duke of Aquitaine. By about 1100 the counts of Toulouse also claimed the titles of duke of Narbonne and marquis of Provence, which made them the most powerful lords between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/toulouse"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toulouse.jpg" alt="Toulouse" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toulouse_-_vue_du_Vieux_Toulouse_depuis_St_Sernin_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Frédéric Neupont, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The lands of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> were among the most urbanised and prosperous in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Toulouse, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, was a major market for the grain and wine of the surrounding plains, and the routes linking the Atlantic, by way of the Garonne, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, Montpellier and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gilles-gard">Saint-Gilles</a> carried cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, salt and leather. Saint-Gilles, the family’s ancestral seat near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> delta, was a busy port and a pilgrimage centre on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, and its abbey church received a celebrated sculpted façade in the twelfth century. Toulouse itself was dominated by the great Romanesque basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-sernin">Saint-Sernin</a>, whose altar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> consecrated in 1096. Society was marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a> among the nobility, much land held as free allods rather than fiefs, and a strong urban patriciate. Southern towns had sizeable Jewish communities, and Occitan, the <em>langue d’oc</em>, was the language of daily life, of much legal writing and of literature. The courts of the counts and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> patronised the troubadours, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peire-vidal">Peire Vidal</a>, a native of Toulouse. From the mid-twelfth century the dualist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathar</a> movement spread among artisans, merchants and lesser nobles of the region, organising its own bishoprics and finding protection among lords who tolerated it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret.jpg" alt="Battle of Muret" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Battle of Muret</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Muret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonimous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> never commanded a standing army; their forces were drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, town militias and, increasingly, hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> known as <em>routiers</em>. Their most celebrated campaign was abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond IV</a>, also known as Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gilles-gard">Saint-Gilles</a>, took the cross in 1095 and led probably the largest contingent of the First Crusade, accompanied by the papal legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adhemar-of-le-puy">Adhemar of Le Puy</a>. He fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and from 1102 besieged Tripoli, building the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pilgrim">Mont Pèlerin</a> outside it; he died there in 1105, and the city fell in 1109 to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-of-toulouse">Bertrand</a>, founding the County of Tripoli. At home the counts fought a long struggle, sometimes called the Great Southern War, against the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> for Provence and the allegiance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> nobility. They also faced the dukes of Aquitaine, who claimed Toulouse by marriage: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-elector-of-hesse">William IX</a> occupied the city twice, and in 1159 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> besieged it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> entered Toulouse to support Raymond V and Henry withdrew. The heaviest blows came during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, when Simon de Montfort defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vi-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Battle of Muret</a> in 1213. Toulouse fought back: in 1218 Simon was killed by a stone from a siege engine while besieging the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis VII of France" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Natalis de Wailly (1805-1886), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The counts ruled less through administration than through lordship, alliance and prestige. Their territory was a patchwork: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, the Toulousain and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigeois">Albigeois</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quercy">Quercy</a> were held fairly directly, but powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trencavel">Trencavel</a> viscounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, the viscounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and the counts of Foix and Comminges shifted allegiance between Toulouse and Barcelona-Aragon as interest dictated. The counts were nominally vassals of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but for long periods had little contact with them. Their power rested on castles, rights of justice, tolls and the revenues of towns, managed by local officers called vicars and bailiffs. Toulouse itself gained a large measure of self-government: by the mid-twelfth century a body of consuls, later known as capitouls, governed the city, and in the decades around 1200 the consulate extended its control over the surrounding countryside and dealt with the counts almost as an equal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diplomatic-marriage">Dynastic marriages</a> tied the house to the great royal families: Raymond V married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vi-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VI</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-england-queen-of-sicily">Joan of England</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>, who brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agenais">Agenais</a> as her dowry. Written law and notarial culture, rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, were strong, as across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midi-france">Midi</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-albigensian-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-of-the-albigensian-crusade.jpg" alt="Song of the Albigensian Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-albigensian-crusade">Song of the Albigensian Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_VIII_Marmande.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The county’s legacy lies above all in the culture of the Occitan south. Under the protection of the counts and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, troubadour lyric became the first great vernacular poetry of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, shaping the tradition of courtly love that passed to the trouvères of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesänger</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and poets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. The ruin of the southern courts during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> scattered the troubadours, yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> kept the tradition alive: in 1323 a group of its citizens founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consistori-del-gai-saber">Consistori del Gai Saber</a>, whose poetry contest, the Jeux floraux, still exists. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> itself left deep marks. It helped bring about the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medieval-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, active in the county from the 1230s, and it shaped the Dominican order, whose founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-dominic">Dominic of Caleruega</a> preached among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> and established his first house of preachers at Toulouse in 1215. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1229 obliged the count to pay for teachers in Toulouse, and the university founded that year still exists. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> East the County of Tripoli, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond IV</a>’s crusade, survived until 1289. The Occitan epic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-albigensian-crusade">Song of the Albigensian Crusade</a></em> and the memory of the Cathars later became central to regional identity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade.jpg" alt="Albigensian Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_massacre_des_Albigeois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The county’s end came through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>. After the papal legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-de-castelnau">Peter of Castelnau</a> was murdered in January 1208, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vi-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VI</a> and proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> and their protectors. In 1209 a northern French army massacred the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, and Simon de Montfort assumed its leadership. Raymond VI, excommunicated and outmanoeuvred, allied with his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a>, but their defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Muret</a> in 1213 left Simon master of the south, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-council-of-the-lateran">Fourth Lateran Council</a> of 1215 granted him most of Raymond’s lands. Raymond VI and his son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vii-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VII</a>, fought back, retaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaucaire">Beaucaire</a> in 1216 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 1217, and Simon’s death in 1218 seemed to reverse the conquest. The intervention of the French crown proved decisive: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france">Louis VIII</a>’s crusade of 1226 broke resistance, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1229 Raymond VII kept a reduced county for life, while his only daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-countess-of-toulouse">Joan</a> was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse of Poitiers</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria">Louis IX</a>. A last revolt in 1242, in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, failed. Raymond VII died in 1249, and when Alphonse and Joan died childless in 1271, the county was united with the royal domain of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> was the greatest principality of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a home of troubadour poetry and the launching point of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>; its destruction in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> under the French crown and helped give rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medieval-inquisition">Inquisition</a> and the Dominican order.</em></p>

